---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const updated = 'November 11, 2026';
const email = 'dinakar91@gmail.com';
---

<Layout
  title="Birdhouse Privacy Policy · Dinakar Tumu"
  description="How Birdhouse Social, an X client for iPhone, iPad and Mac, handles your data."
>
  <article class="container prose">
    <h1>Birdhouse privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="muted small">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      Birdhouse Social ("Birdhouse") is an app for reading and posting to X on iPhone, iPad
      and Mac. It is made by Dinakar Tumu, an independent developer. Birdhouse is not
      affiliated with or endorsed by X Corp.
    </p>

    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      Birdhouse has no server of its own. It talks directly to X, and everything it keeps
      stays on your device. There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking, and I never see
      your account, your posts or your messages.
    </p>

    <h2>Signing in</h2>
    <p>
      You sign in on X's own sign-in page, shown inside the app. Birdhouse never sees or
      stores your password. After you sign in, the session cookies X issues are saved in
      your device's Keychain and are sent only to X.
    </p>

    <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Timelines, posts, profiles, lists and notifications fetched from X, cached so the app opens quickly.</li>
      <li>Your drafts, mute rules and settings.</li>
      <li>Images and videos, cached for display.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This data is stored in the app's private storage. Signing out removes your session,
      and deleting the app removes everything it stored.
    </p>

    <h2>Who Birdhouse talks to</h2>
    <p>
      Birdhouse connects only to X and its media servers (x.com, twimg.com and t.co) to
      load your timeline, post on your behalf and fetch images and videos. What you do
      there is covered by <a href="https://x.com/en/privacy">X's privacy policy</a>.
      Opening a link from a post opens that website, which has its own policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Camera and photos</h2>
    <p>
      If you allow it, Birdhouse uses the camera to take a photo for a post, and adds to your
      photo library only the photos and videos you choose to save. Media you attach is sent
      only to X, as part of your post.
    </p>

    <h2>Crash reports and beta feedback</h2>
    <p>
      Birdhouse doesn't include any crash-reporting or analytics tools. If you share crash
      reports or feedback with developers through Apple (for example in TestFlight), Apple
      passes them to me under <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/">Apple's privacy policy</a>.
      I use them only to fix bugs.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Birdhouse is not directed at children, and follows X's own age requirements.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a new date.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about privacy? Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .container { max-width: 40rem; }
  h1 { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 2.25rem 0 0.75rem; }
  .small { font-size: 0.85rem; margin-top: 0; }
  .prose a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .prose ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; }
</style>
